    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Have you struggled with this kind of restlessness and needing to move around since you were a child in school, or is this something that started more recently?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, wow, yeah, no, this is definitely not a new thing. Not at all. I mean, if you asked my mom, she'd probably tell you I came out of the womb trying to run a marathon. But yeah, school was... well, it was a disaster, honestly. Even way back in elementary school, I was always *that* kid. You know the one. The one who just could not, for the life of her, stay in her assigned seat. It was like those little plastic chairs they had were made of lava or something. I'd be up sharpening my pencil, or going to the trash can, or just wandering over to look at the class pet—we had a hamster named Barnaby in third grade, and I swear I spent more time at his cage than at my desk. \n\nAnd it wasn't just the moving around, either. I was constantly getting in trouble for talking. I'd just talk right over the teacher. Like, she'd be in the middle of explaining fractions or something, and a thought would pop into my head, and I just had to say it right then and there. I couldn't hold it in. It was like a sneeze, you know? You can't just hold in a sneeze. So I'd just blurt things out, completely interrupting the whole lesson. My backpack was basically just a delivery service for notes from the teacher to my parents. 'She's disruptive,' 'she won't stay seated,' 'she blurts out answers without raising her hand.' It was constant. So, yeah, this whole 'not being able to stay put and just doing things before I think' thing... it's been the story of my life since forever. It's just that now, instead of annoying a teacher, I'm apparently a hazard to myself and others around heavy machinery.",
      "new_confirmed_symptoms": [
