    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Outside of the physical restlessness, do you often have trouble keeping your focus on tasks that require sustained mental effort?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S002",
      "patient_response": "Oh my gosh, yes. It's not just the walking away thing, it's... well, it's anything that makes me sit still and actually use my brain for more than two seconds. \n\nLike, at work, they have us do these inventory logs sometimes, right? And lately, they've also been having me read over these CNC training pages because they were talking about cross-training me on the new machines before this whole wandering-off issue got so bad. Anyway, with both of those things, the logs and the training manuals, I really do try. I sit down and I tell myself, 'Okay, you are going to read this, you are going to do this right.' I start out really trying so hard to just focus. \n\nBut honestly, after just a few minutes—maybe not even a few minutes, maybe like sixty seconds—the numbers on the inventory sheets or the words on the training pages just completely blur together. I'll be staring right at the paper, moving my eyes down the lines, but I'll get to the bottom of the page and realize I didn't take any of it in. Nothing. It's just completely blank up here. So then I have to keep going back to the top and starting over, over and over again, because none of it stuck the first time, or the second time. And it's so incredibly frustrating because it's just basic reading and counting, you know? It shouldn't be this massive, impossible chore, but I just can't keep my head locked onto the page no matter how hard I try to force myself. I just end up staring at a blurry wall of text.",
